Now take a set of rectangular axes fixed in the planet, and let <math>\text{M}_1r</math>, <math>\text{M}_2r</math>, <math>\text{M}_3r</math> be the co-ordinates of the satellite referred thereto; and let <math>\xi\rho</math>, <math>\eta\rho</math>, <math>\zeta\rho</math> be the co-ordinates of the particle <math>P</math> whose radius vector is <math>\rho</math>. Then the component accelerations for reducing the planet's centre to rest are <math>-m\text{M}_1/r^2</math>, <math>-m\text{M}_2/r^2</math>, <math>-m\text{M}_3/r^2</math>; and since these are the differential coefficients with respect to <math>\rho\xi</math>, <math>\rho\eta</math>, <math>\rho\zeta</math> of the function
{{block center|<math>-\frac{m\rho}{r^2}(\text{M}_1\xi+\text{M}_2\eta+\text{M}_3\zeta),</math>}}
and since <math>\cos z=\text{M}_1\xi+\text{M}_2\eta+\text{M}_3\zeta</math>, it follows that the potential of the forces by which the planet's centre is to be reduced to rest is
{{block center|<math>-\frac{m\rho}{r^2}\cos z.</math>}}

