In the case considered in § 11, <math>i = 2</math> and <math>S_i</math> becomes the second zonal harmonic <math>\cos^2 z - \tfrac{1}{3}</math>.

The theory of harmonic analysis tells us that the form of the ocean, when in equilibrium, must be given by the equation
{{block center|<math>\rho = a + e_i S_i.</math> (4)}}
Our problem is to evaluate <math>e_i</math>. We know that the external potential of a layer of matter, of depth <math>e_i S_i</math> and density <math>\sigma</math>, has the value
{{block center|<math>\frac{4\pi\sigma a}{2i + 1} \left(\frac{a}{\rho}\right)^{i+1} e_i S_i.</math>}}
Hence the whole potential externally to the planet and up to its surface is
{{block center|<math>\frac{M}{\rho} + \frac{3ma^2}{2r^3} \left(\frac{\rho}{a}\right)^i S_i + \frac{4\pi\sigma a}{2i + 1} \left(\frac{a}{\rho}\right)^{i+1} e_i S_i.</math> (5)}}

The first and most important term is the potential of the planet, the second that of the disturbing force, and the third that of the departure from sphericity.

